## Real Price Example: Deep-Cut Trap in Action

Consider two 1ct G-VS2 round diamonds on Blue Nile:

- **Stone A**: GIA Excellent, depth 61.2%, table 56% → priced at [$3,230](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — optimal proportions, face-up at 6.40mm
- **Stone B**: GIA Very Good, depth 64.8%, table 57% → priced at ~$2,750 — $480 cheaper but looks like a smaller stone at ~6.1mm face-up

More 1ct G-VS2 GIA Excellent options with correct depth (all 59.5–62.0%):
- [$3,240](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090694?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — second option at nearly identical price
- [$3,370](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29112616?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — third option, slightly higher clarity positioning
- [$3,490](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28941971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — fourth option at the same G-VS2 grade

All four stones are GIA Excellent and face-up at approximately 6.40mm. The $480 saved on a GIA Very Good at 64.8% depth is illusory: the stone looks like a $2,200 stone because its face-up size matches a 0.83ct well-cut diamond.

The GIA Very Good grade discloses the depth issue without explicitly warning the buyer that face-up appearance is affected. Buyers who do not check depth % discover this only after purchase.

The conclusion: depth percentage is not optional reading. It is a first-tier filter, not an advanced one.

