## Shallow Diamonds: The Other Extreme

Depth below 58.5% is also problematic, though less common. Shallow stones produce a "fish-eye" or "nail-head" effect: a dark, dead circle visible at the center of the table where light exits through the bottom rather than returning through the crown.

This happens because the pavilion angle on a shallow stone is too flat — instead of reflecting light back up through the table, the facets deflect light through the sides of the stone. The result is a visually larger diameter (shallow stones measure wide per carat) but a dark center that kills the stone's apparent brilliance.

GIA Excellent does not include stones with fish-eye effects — the grade parameters prevent this. But in stones below GIA Excellent with depths under 58%, fish-eye is a real risk worth verifying via video.

---

## The Depth Percentage Buying Checklist

Use this checklist before purchasing any round brilliant diamond.

**Step 1: Apply the GIA Excellent or Ideal filter on Blue Nile.**
This eliminates GIA Very Good and GIA Good cut grades, which contain the deepest deep-cut stones (63%–70% depth range). The Excellent filter brings you into the 59–62.3% range.

**Step 2: Check the depth % on the GIA certificate.**
Target: 59.5%–62.0%. If the depth is outside this range, ask yourself why. A stone at 62.2% is fine — it is at the upper end of Excellent but still within range.

A stone at 58.7% is approaching shallow territory. Any stone at 62.4%+ is outside GIA Excellent — reject it.

**Step 3: Check the table %.**
Target: 54%–58%. Table and depth work together. If both are in range, the stone's core proportions are correct.

If depth is correct but table is 61%, the stone has insufficient fire regardless of its depth performance.

**Step 4: Check the pavilion angle.**
Target: 40.6°–41.0°. This angle, combined with depth %, determines where light exits.

A stone at 61% depth with a 41.5° pavilion angle will underperform a stone at 61.5% depth with a 40.8° pavilion angle. When depth is correct, pavilion angle is the tiebreaker.

**Step 5: Watch the HD video.**
After confirming proportions, watch the Blue Nile HD video for your shortlisted stones. Two stones with identical depth % and pavilion angle can look different on video due to minor crown angle and crown height variations.

The video catches these differences. A stone that looks bright and even on video with correct certificate proportions is a strong buy signal.

**Step 6: Confirm no dark center in the video.**
In a correctly proportioned stone (depth 59.5–62%), no dark center should be visible in the face-up video. If you see a gray or dark zone in the center of the table, the stone may have proportions that interact poorly despite being within the Excellent range. Reject it and move to the next stone.

Most buyers complete this checklist in 3–5 minutes once familiar with where to find each data point on Blue Nile. The first time takes 10–15 minutes; the second stone takes 5 minutes; by the third stone, it becomes automatic.

Running this checklist takes 10 minutes per stone. It eliminates The Deep-Cut Trap before purchase and identifies the top-performing stones within the GIA Excellent range. This is the process that separates buyers who are happy with their diamonds from buyers who wonder why their stone looks "off" after six months of wearing it.

---

## Depth Percentage in Lab-Grown vs Natural Diamonds

Depth percentage standards are identical for lab-grown and natural round diamonds. GIA Excellent cut grading for lab-grown natural rounds uses the same proportion models. IGI Excellent cut grading (the most common lab-grown certification) uses similar but slightly more permissive criteria.

For IGI Excellent cut lab-grown rounds, still check depth: 59–63% is acceptable for IGI Excellent, but target 59.5–62% for optimal performance within IGI standards.

See our [lab grown round diamond guide](/lab-grown-round-diamond) for complete lab-grown grading context.

---

## Depth and the Setting: How Prong Count Affects Light Return

Setting prong count does not change depth percentage — but it does affect how light enters the stone, which interacts with depth performance.

A 4-prong setting exposes more of the girdle and crown, allowing light to enter from more angles. This benefits correctly proportioned stones (59.5–62% depth) because light enters freely and returns efficiently through the table. In a deep stone (65% depth), a 4-prong setting does not fix the depth problem — the pavilion is still too steep to return light correctly.

A 6-prong setting covers slightly more of the stone and can create minor shadows at the prong bases. For correctly proportioned stones, this is a minimal effect. For deep stones, it adds to the visual darkness at the stone's center.

Bezel settings surround the girdle with metal, which blocks light entry from the sides. Bezel-set deep stones perform worse than prong-set deep stones because the bezel adds another layer of light restriction to an already compromised light path. For buyers choosing bezel settings: correct depth is even more critical, not less, because the setting provides less latitude for proportion variation.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** Depth percentage is the proportion that buyers most consistently ignore — and the one that most directly determines face-up size and brilliance. My rule: never buy a round diamond without checking depth, regardless of cut grade. GIA Excellent guarantees you are in the correct range; the target range of 59.5–62.0% ensures you are at the best tier within that range. A 1ct round at 65% depth is not a 1ct diamond in any meaningful visual sense — it is a diamond that weighs 1ct but shows like 0.82ct. At $3,230 per carat, that is an expensive lesson. Filter for Ideal/Excellent cut, then manually verify depth before adding to your cart.

