## The GIA Proportion Diagram: How to Read the Cross-Section

Every GIA Diamond Grading Report for a round brilliant includes a proportion diagram — a cross-section showing the stone's specific measurements. This diagram is available on Blue Nile's certificate page for every listed stone.

**What the diagram shows:**

The diagram is a vertical cross-section of the diamond. The widest point is the girdle. Above the girdle is the crown (upper half); below is the pavilion (lower half).

The depth percentage is the total height of the cross-section divided by the girdle diameter.

**What to look for:**

In a correctly proportioned stone at 61% depth, the pavilion is roughly twice the height of the crown — a balanced profile. In a deep stone at 65%, the pavilion extends visibly further below the girdle relative to the crown height. You can see the depth problem in the diagram before reading the number.

**Red flags in the diagram:**

- Pavilion noticeably taller than crown (indicates deep stone)
- Very thick girdle band (indicates extra weight hiding in the band)
- Culet dot visible (indicates a larger culet than "None")

Reading the diagram alongside the proportion numbers gives you two independent ways to confirm whether a stone falls in the correct range.

