## Depth Percentage and the GIA Grading Report: What to Read

The GIA Diamond Grading Report lists depth percentage under the Proportions section. For round brilliants, the report shows:

| Item | Location | What to Look For |
|---|---|---|
| Depth % | Proportions section | 59.5–62.0% target |
| Table % | Proportions section | 54–58% target |
| Crown angle | Proportions section | 34.0°–35.0° |
| Pavilion angle | Proportions section | 40.6°–41.0° |
| Girdle | Proportions section | Thin to Slightly Thick |
| Culet | Proportions section | None or Pointed |
| Cut grade | Grade section | Excellent |

The GIA report includes a diagram of the stone's proportions — a cross-section showing crown height, pavilion depth, table width, and girdle thickness to scale. If a stone has a 64% total depth, the diagram shows a noticeably tall pavilion relative to the table. You can see visually where the depth is hiding before you buy.

Blue Nile shows GIA report data on every stone's detail page. Scroll down to "Cut Details" or look for the GIA certificate link. The full proportion data is available without calling a sales representative.

---

## Depth vs. Table: The Two-Number System

Depth percentage alone does not tell the complete story. It works together with table percentage to determine where a stone sits on the fire-vs-brilliance spectrum.

**Fire-optimized stones (more rainbow sparkle):**

- Table 53–55% + Depth 59.5–61.5% → maximum fire, lower brilliance
- These stones create strong colored light dispersion (fire) but less total white light return (brilliance)
- Best for buyers who want visible colored flashes in candlelight or restaurant lighting

**Balance stones (most versatile):**

- Table 55–57% + Depth 60–62% → balanced fire and brilliance
- These are the most-recommended proportions for everyday wear across all lighting environments
- The 1ct G-VS2 benchmark stones ([#29090690](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at $3,230) fall in this range

**Brilliance-optimized stones (more white sparkle):**

- Table 57–58% + Depth 61–62.3% → maximum brilliance, reduced fire
- Higher brilliance means more total light return; stone appears brighter overall but with fewer colored flashes
- Best for buyers whose primary aesthetic is maximum white sparkle

The Deep-Cut Trap lives at depth above 62.5% combined with any table % — at that depth, neither fire nor brilliance is optimized because light is not returning through the table correctly regardless of table size.

> **Farzana's Two-Number Rule:** I check depth % first. If it falls outside 59.5–62.0%, the stone is eliminated regardless of other specs. Then I check table %. If both numbers are in range, I move to pavilion angle. These three numbers together eliminate 80% of GIA Excellent stones that perform below the grade level — and they take 60 seconds to verify on the GIA report. Skipping this step means accepting whatever the market provides. At $3,230 per stone, that is not an acceptable shortcut.

---

> **Farzana's Buying Protocol at the 1ct Tier:** Every 1ct diamond purchase I evaluate goes through the same depth check: is depth between 59.5% and 62.0%? If yes, proceed. If no, eliminate and move to the next stone. This eliminates The Deep-Cut Trap before I ever watch the video or evaluate color. The $480 difference between a correctly proportioned stone and a deep-cut stone is not recoverable after purchase — it is locked in by the cutter's decision to preserve rough at the expense of face-up appearance. Depth is the first filter, not the last.

---

## Frequently Asked Questions (continued)

### What is the best depth percentage for a 1ct round diamond?

The optimal depth range is 59.5%–62.0% for maximum light performance. This range produces the most balanced combination of brilliance, fire, and face-up size.

Stones at 61.0%–61.8% within this range consistently test highest in independent light return analysis. GIA Excellent accepts 59.0%–62.3% — the 59.5–62.0% sub-range represents the top tier within GIA Excellent.

